1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,560
What keeps us healthy and happy as we go through life.
在人生旅程中，是什么让我们保持健康和快乐？

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:14,119
If you were going to invest now in your future best self where would you put your time and your energy.
如果现在要为未来最好的自己投资，你会把时间和精力放在哪里？

3
00:00:14,119 --> 00:00:21,840
There was a recent survey of millennials asking them what their most important life goals were.
最近有一项针对千禧一代的调查，询问他们最重要的人生目标是什么。

4
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:27,960
And over 80 said that a major life goal for them was to get rich.
其中超过八成的人说，致富是他们的一项主要人生目标。

5
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:39,280
And another 50 of those same young adults said that another major life goal was to become famous.
同一批年轻人中，另有一半说，成名是他们的另一项主要目标。

6
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:47,869
And we're constantly told to lean in to work to push harder and achieve more.
我们不断被告知，要全身投入工作，更加努力，取得更多成就。

7
00:00:47,879 --> 00:00:53,520
We're given the impression that these are the things that we need to go after in order to have a good life.
这些信息使我们产生一种印象：为了过上美好生活，我们需要追求的就是这些东西。

8
00:00:53,520 --> 00:01:05,280
Pictures of entire lives of the choices that people make and how those choices work out for them those pictures are almost impossible to get.
要得到整个人生的全貌，了解人们的选择及其结果，几乎是不可能的。

9
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:11,680
Most of what we know about human life we know from asking people to remember the past.
我们对人生的认识，大多来自请人们回忆过去。

10
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:20,240
And as we know hindsight is anything but 2020 we forget vast amounts of what happens to us in life.
而我们知道，事后回忆并不总是清楚准确；生活中发生的很多事情，我们都会忘记。

11
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,119
And sometimes memory is downright creative.
有时，记忆甚至会发挥十足的创造力。

12
00:01:24,119 --> 00:01:31,069
But what if we could watch entire lives as they unfold through time.
但假如我们能随着时间，观察完整的人生逐步展开呢？

13
00:01:31,079 --> 00:01:42,600
What if we could study people from the time that they were teenagers all the way into old age to see what really keeps people happy and healthy.
假如我们能从青少年时期开始，一直研究他们到老年，以了解到底是什么让人保持快乐与健康，会怎样呢？

14
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:52,680
We did that the harvard study of adult development may be the longest study of adult life that's ever been done.
我们真的这样做了。哈佛成人发展研究，可能是迄今持续时间最长的成人生活研究。

15
00:01:52,680 --> 00:02:04,920
For 75 years we've tracked the lives of 724 men year after year asking about their work their home lives their health.
七十五年来，我们年复一年追踪七百二十四名男性的生活，询问工作、家庭生活和健康情况。

16
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:12,480
And of course asking all along the way without knowing how their life stories were going to turn out.
当然，我们一路询问时，并不知道他们的人生故事最终会如何发展。

17
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:20,200
Studies like this are exceedingly rare almost all projects of this kind fall apart within a decade.
这样的研究极其罕见。这类项目几乎都会在十年内中断。

18
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:26,159
Because too many people drop out of the study or funding for the research dries up.
原因是太多人退出研究，或研究经费用尽。

19
00:02:26,159 --> 00:02:33,319
Or the researchers get distracted or they die and nobody moves the ball further down the field.
或者研究者分心、去世，此后也没有人继续推动研究。

20
00:02:33,319 --> 00:02:41,680
But through a combination of luck and the persistence of several generations of researchers this study has survived.
但依靠运气，以及几代研究者坚持不懈的共同作用，这项研究延续了下来。

21
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:49,720
About 60 of our original 724 men are still alive still participating in the study.
最初的七百二十四名男性中，大约六十人当时仍在世，也仍然参与研究。

22
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:58,710
Most of them in their 9 s and we are now beginning to study the more than 2 000 children of these men.
他们大多已九十多岁；我们也正开始研究这些男性的两千多名子女。

23
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,440
And I'm the fourth director of the study.
而我是这项研究的第四任负责人。
